  "query_preview": "..Primary focus continues to be on warm/dry through the\nend of the week along with the uncertainty of equatorial \ntropical storms and subsequent moisture transport into the N/Pac. \nStarting off, for the end of the week clusters still show strong \nagreement in a ridge dominating the western seaboard, limiting \nmajor surface lows from moving into the Gulf and serving as the \nsource for abnormally warm temperatures aloft. LREF 850mb \ntemperature histograms have shifted to more normal distribution, \ntranslating to increased confidence for 12C to 14C at 5000ft by \nThur/Fri. For folks at sea level, this means warm temperatures, \nwith afternoon highs likely in the mid-70s to mid-80s, depending \non where you are in the Panhandle. Thunderstorms might make an \nappearance Thur/Fri across the central and southern area; however,\nthis will ultimately depend on where the ridge axis sets up and a\nshortwave moving down from Canada along the coast range, \nsomething to watch.\n\nMoving through the weekend guidance breaks down the ridge\nsuggesting cooler temperatures and some moisture; however,\nguidance continues to break down the ridge too soon. Thinking we\nhold on to warmer temperatures into Saturday, with cooler \ntemperatures and moisture arriving by late Sunday and/or start of \nnext week. With that said, there are a variety of outcomes in \nvarious ensemble system solutions; current forecast Monday \nafternoon sticks with troughing setting up in the Gulf and an \nincrease in clouds and light rain. \n \nFor the maritime environment, anticipate northwest winds to\ncontinue along the coast, with daily sea breezes moving through \nthe inside, peak afternoon speeds likely moderate to fresh \nbreezes. By Sunday/Monday northwest winds along the coast give way\nto southerlies as a series of lows moves into the Gulf. Will need\nto watch for the potential for fresh to strong breezes along the \nnorthern coast.",
